Z||000000||fierosound||01-13-2000||10:44 PM||||Found this ad online in Vancouver Buy & Sell edition.  I guess he's "seen the light".  The only problem is...  <BR>why would a Fiero owner want a Mazda???<P><BR>MAZDA RX7 GSLSE, blk w red lthr int, exc cond, fuel inj 2nd generation mtr, lots of new pts,<BR>must sell $1700 obo or trade for Pontiac Fiero. <p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 01-13-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||Standard||01-14-2000||12:30 AM||||I wouldn't mind having an rx7.. nice looking cars and the wankel's a pretty cool engine.  I like unique.  I'd still have a fiero, though||||reg||7
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||01-14-2000||03:05 AM||||I like Mazdas.. the RX7, MX3 and the Mitsubishi 3000 GT are my favorite japanese cars.. GMs are still my favorite however.<P>An RX7 for $1700 is a pretty good deal IMHO.. no mention of what year the car is tho!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||01-14-2000||09:21 AM||||I believe the GSL-SE was an '85. Nice one, too. All the toys. Also had the big (13B) motor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||01-14-2000||11:02 AM||||I almost bought an '83 GSL before I got my first fiero (also my first car!)  The owner basicly sold it out from under me.  I am glad he did though, otherwise I would never have been introduced to fieros via my '84 SC!  The GSL-SE was produced in '84 and '85.  It had the bigger engine, the 13B, which was standard in the second gen. car.  The GSL-SE is the hot ticket, if you are going to burn rice.  I wonder what would happen if you put a 13B turbo wankel in a fiero.........||||reg||7
Z||000005||bHooper||01-14-2000||11:52 AM||||i had a 2nd generation rx-7... automatic.  i bought it after the trany went in my fiero, and i was temperarily fed up with manual transmisions.  anyway, that car was a complete dog!  a wankel and a auto are not a good mix.  whent the 3rd generation rx-7 came out, i almost bought one of those... very nice cars, even now!||||reg||13
Z||000006||Raydar||01-14-2000||02:10 PM||||Shiner... Wondered about that very thing. (13B Rotary in a Fiero.) I'll bet it's been done, somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||01-14-2000||05:08 PM||||I used to work for a Mazda dealershiop from 81' to 86.  Part of that time I did the pre-dealership inspections on them.  The RX-7 was my favorite at that time though I ended up buying an RX-2.  Those things can scream!  It's the only car that I never blew out the transmission and there was over 100,000 miles on it.  That's saying a lot back then.  I used to burn rubber everywhere.  I used to race around a lake where I used to live(lake Berryessa CA).  With struts clear around, that thing could handle a corner well.  I was looking for an RX-7 when I came across my 86 GT.  What a find!  It was a donated car to a chairity because the front manifold messed up on him and he thought he was getting to old to climb in and out of it.  I'm having back spasms right now and I'll still crawl into my car. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000008||mshill||01-14-2000||06:48 PM||||I had an RX-7 and before that a Triumph GT6.  The RX-7 was a fun car but not really a sports car.  It handled like a pig, but those rotary engines will run forever.  The GT6 had fantastic power, but was so light in the back end that you couldn't keep traction on the rear wheels.  For what it's worth my 86SE with 189K miles on it would have probably outperformed both of these cars when they were brand new.<BR>||||reg||1
